      "writtenOn": "2023-03-02T10:10:19Z",
      "side": 1,
      "message": "This should probably be called isDefaultNetworkMigrationEnabled. The corresponding setter should be changed to setDefaultNetworkMigrationEnabled.",
      "range": {
        "startLine": 48,
        "startChar": 36,
        "endLine": 48,
        "endChar": 67
      },
      "revId": "db917647324474557b607d59c49bf40a6fe2f059",
      "serverId": "85c56323-6fa9-3386-8a01-6480fb634889"
    },
    {
      "unresolved": true,
      "key": {
        "uuid": "5e539768_a6d60373",
        "filename": "Tethering/common/TetheringLib/cronet_enabled/api/current.txt",
        "patchSetId": 8
      },
      "lineNbr": 56,
      "author": {
        "id": 1000835
      },
      "writtenOn": "2023-03-02T10:10:19Z",
      "side": 1,
      "message": "The fact that the getter returns a @Nullable Boolean implies that this is actually a tristate: true, false, unspecified. Given that, I think it should be possible to *set* the unspecified value as well. So these should also take @Nullable Booleans.\n\nAn alternative would be to follow go/android-api-guidelines#classes-avoid-optional and do something like:\n- hasDefaultNetworkMigrationEnabled returns whether the migration boolean is set or unset\n- isdefaultNetworkMigrationEnabled returns whether migration is enabled or not, and throws IllegalStateException if the above returns false.\n\nPersonally I feel that @Nullable boolean for both getter and setter is better. The above example with the two methods seems very confusing to me.",
      "range": {
        "startLine": 56,
        "startChar": 102,
        "endLine": 56,
        "endChar": 109
      },
      "revId": "db917647324474557b607d59c49bf40a6fe2f059",
      "serverId": "85c56323-6fa9-3386-8a01-6480fb634889"
    },
    {
      "unresolved": true,
      "key": {
